​​​​Consider an array $\mathrm{X}$ that contains $\mathrm{n}$ positive integers. A subarray of $\mathrm{X}$ is defined to be a sequence of array locations with consecutive indices.
   The $\mathrm{C}$ code snippet given below has been written to compute the length of the longest subarray of $\mathrm{X}$ that contains at most two distinct integers. The code has two missing expressions labelled $\text{(P)}$ and $\text{(Q)}$.


int first=0, second=0, len1=0, len2=0, maxlen=0;
for (int i=0; i < n; i++) {
    if (X[i] == first) {
        len2++; len1++;
    } else if (X[i] == second) {
        len2++;
        len1 = (P); 

        second = first;
    } else {
        len2 = (Q);

        len1 = 1; second = first;
    }
    if (len2 > maxlen) {
        maxlen = len2;
    }
    first = X[i];
}

   Which one of the following options gives the CORRECT missing expressions?


   (

    Hint:

   At the end of the $i$-th iteration, the value of $len 1$ is the length of the longest subarray ending with $\mathrm{X}[i]$ that contains all equal values, and $len 2$ is the length of the longest subarray ending with $\mathrm{X}[i]$ that contains at most two distinct values.)

explanation:
In the given program, we keep track of the at most two distinct values allowed in the

    subarray

   using the variables $\bf{first}$ & $\bf{second}$.


   The variable $\bf{first}$ stores the


     latest


   (or


     the second


   ) distinct value in the subarray,


   while the variable $\bf{second}$ stores the


     second latest


   (or


     the first


   ) distinct value we see in the subarray.


   The code with explanation,


for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {

  /* as long as the current digit is same as the previous digit
  we should keep incrementing len1 and len2. 
  NOTE: we have at most two distinct digits in our subarray. */
  if (X[i] == first) {
    len2++; len1++;
  } 

  /* if the current digit is different than the preceding digit but 
  same as the first distinct digit in the subarray then
  we should keep incrementing len2 and reset len1 to 1,
  as a/c to the hint given len1 is supposed to be the 
  length of the longest subarray ending with X[i] that contains all equal values. 
  NOTE: we have exactly two distinct digits in our subarray */
  else if (X[i] == second) {
    len2++;
    len1 = 1; // (P)
    second = first;
  }

  /* the moment we see a third distinct digit in the array 
  means that we have to reset the subarray to use only 
  the last two distinct values encountered and so,
  we should make the variable len2 = len1+1 and len1 = 1,
  as a/c to the hint given len2 is supposed to be the 
  length of the longest subarray ending with X[i] that contains at most two distinct values. */
  else {
    len2 = len1 + 1; // (Q)
    len1 = 1;
    second = first;
  }

  if (len2 > maxlen) {
    maxlen = len2;
  }
  first = X[i];
}

   $ \bf \therefore Ans = B. $
Correct answer: B


