​​​​​Consider a multi-threaded program with two threads $\mathrm{T} 1$ and $\mathrm{T} 2$. The threads share two semaphores: $s1$ (initialized to $1$) and $s2$ (initialized to $0$). The threads also share a global variable $\mathrm{x}$ (initialized to $0$). The threads execute the code shown below.


//code of T 1
wait (s1);
x = x+1;
print (x);
wait (s2);
signal(s1);

// code of T2
wait (s1);
x= x+1;
print (x) ;
signal (s2);
signal (s1);

   Which of the following outcomes is/are possible when threads $\mathrm{T} 1$ and $\mathrm{T} 2$ execute concurrently?

explanation:
Detailed Video Solution:


    Semaphore question - GATE CSE 2024


   Correct Answer:- B , C .


   Since initially $S_1 = 1$ & both the threads are performing $wait$ operation on $S_1,$ So, only one thread can succeed & run.


   If thread $T_1$ runs first, then $T_1$ gets blocked when it executes $wait(S_2)$. Also, $T_2$ cannot run as it would get blocked when it executes $wait(S_1)$. So, deadlock occurs.
Option B,C is Ans


